  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 204 PM EDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nRest of Today-Wednesday...An upper level trough digs through the\nSoutheast today, slowing and reaching north Florida by Wednesday\nnight. High moisture (PWATs 2-2.3\") and the unsettled pattern will\ncontinue to produce scattered to numerous showers and storms\n(40-70%), especially during the afternoons. The sea breeze will \nbe the main driver for convection, with onshore flow pushing \nactivity westward into the early evening hours. A few strong\nstorms, with gusts up to 50 mph, cannot be ruled out, but\nwarming 500 mb temperatures (to as warm as -5 to -6 C) will limit\nthe threat overall. Instead, locally heavy rainfall of 1-3\" looks\nto be the primary threat, with ample moisture to tap into. Drier \nconditions develop through the evening, with offshore steering \nflow keep a majority of persistent overnight convection over the \nAtlantic waters.\n\nWinds increase to around 10 mph in the afternoons, but become\nlight and variable overnight. Will see winds back NNE through the\nday on Wednesday as a weak cool front approaches. Patchy fog\ncannot be ruled out, mainly over the interior and along the\nTreasure Coast, late and into the early morning hours, though \nchances remain very low (10%). High temperatures stay warm in the\nupper 80s to lower 90s, with lows in the mid-70s.\n\nThursday-Friday...A low pressure system deepens off of the\nNortheast US coast Thursday into Friday, dragging the\naforementioned weakening cool front southward through central\nFlorida. Higher coverage of showers and storms is forecast once\nagain for Thursday along and ahead of the front (PoPs 50-70%).\nHowever, drier air begins to filter into the area on Friday,\nlimiting PoPs to 10-30%. The highest PoPs are forecast along the\ncoast and south, nearest to the front. Breezy to windy NNE winds\ndevelop through the day on Thursday as the pressure gradient\ntightens. Sustained winds 15-20 mph, with gusts 25-30 mph then \nspread southward on Friday. The highest winds will be along the \ncoast, especially north of Cape Canaveral. Temperatures begin to\ndecrease, with highs in the upper 80s to lower 90s Thursday\nremaining in the 80s Friday. \n\nSaturday-Tuesday...An early taste of fall, at least by Florida\nstandards, is forecast this weekend into early next week. Drier \nair lingers over the Florida peninsula, maintaining low rain\nchances across southern portions of the forecast area (30% or\nless) and below mentionable PoPs elsewhere. Most pleasant of all,\nperhaps, will be the lower humidity. Max heat indices will be near\nto only a few degrees higher than forecast highs, which reach the\nmid-80s. Lows are forecast to fall into the 60s. Breezy and gusty\nNE winds Saturday slacken into the new week, remaining onshore \noverall.",
